1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,013
One disease killed an estimated three
hundred million people in the twentieth

2
00:00:05,013 --> 00:00:06,123
century alone.

3
00:00:06,414 --> 00:00:07,884
That disease was smallpox.

4
00:00:08,229 --> 00:00:10,619
For centuries, no one knew how to stop it.

5
00:00:10,810 --> 00:00:13,740
Then an English country doctor
noticed something strange.

6
00:00:14,121 --> 00:00:18,291
Milkmaids who caught the mild cowpox
did not seem to catch smallpox.

7
00:00:18,642 --> 00:00:20,112
His name was Edward Jenner.

8
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:25,437
In May 1796, he took a tiny bit
of cowpox from a milkmaid's sore.

9
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:29,323
He put it on a scratch on an
eight-year-old boy, James Phipps.

10
00:00:29,444 --> 00:00:31,874
The boy had a mild illness,
and then recovered.

11
00:00:32,224 --> 00:00:34,914
Weeks later,
Jenner exposed him to smallpox.

12
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:36,680
The boy did not fall ill.

13
00:00:36,936 --> 00:00:40,526
The body had learned to fight it,
without suffering the real disease.

14
00:00:41,077 --> 00:00:43,167
That is how every vaccine works today.

15
00:00:43,467 --> 00:00:46,737
It shows your immune system
a harmless sign of the enemy.

16
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:49,028
Your body remembers it, and is ready.

17
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:52,649
The word vaccine even comes
from the Latin word for cow.

18
00:00:52,959 --> 00:00:57,369
In 1980, the World Health
Organization declared smallpox gone.

19
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:00,710
It is the first human
disease we ever wiped out.

